							</span>
						</td>
						<td valign="top" width="85%" height="100%">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="middle"><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1016.msg10107#msg10107"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/post/xx.gif" alt="" border="0" /></a></td>
								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1016.msg10107#msg10107">Re: Future of EQ</a></b><br />
									<span class="smalltext">&#171; <b>Reply #3 on:</b> August 16, 2004, 09:47:48 am &#187;</span></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							There is a slight difference caused by the steady alienation of the oldest, most reliable and in general, best, players of Everquest. In part this is simply because the game is old, and nothing really new has happened in ages. I mean everything has been dumbed down and challenge essentially means figuring out an encounter (but even in PoP this was more like &#039;figure out how to get through a blind corner... you have to go at full speed. Yes, you WILL fail the first time, but after you know what to expect, it&#039;s usually fairly trivial. Sometimes there&#039;s many corners in a row, but that still doesn&#039;t equal true difficulty)... I know EQ engine way better than I know most programs I wrote myself over 3 years ago etc.<br /><br />EQs worst enemy is its own age. It has nothing to do with graphics, it has everything to do with being WAY too used to it and it getting steadily easier. The organizational side has been trivialized starting from group buffs, to mass group buffs, to res sticks, to /raid, to /consent raid to /rsay to channels to whatever. There&#039;s no challenge, there&#039;s only the wheel where you run trying to keep up.<br /><br />Of course it&#039;s quite reasonable to wonder if any game can really re-capture this original audience, and it&#039;s not so much EQ getting old, as it&#039;s the whole genre getting old. Basically I fear myself that there needs to be something revolutionary to truly recapture my audience. WoW doesn&#039;t even TRY to do anything revolutionary, which means it might not really hold my attention. Vanguard... now that&#039;s the first one I&#039;m truly interested in.
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" colspan="2" class="smalltext">
								</td>
							</tr><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
								</td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
									<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/ip.gif" alt="" border="0" /> Logged
								</td>
							</tr></table>
						</td>
					</tr>
				</table>
			</td></tr>
		</table>
	</td></tr>
	<tr><td style="padding: 1px 1px 0 1px;">
		<a name="msg10108"></a>
		<table cellpadding="3" cellspacing="0" border="0" width="100%">
			<tr><td class="windowbg">
				<table width="100%" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0">
					<tr>
						<td valign="top" width="15%" rowspan="2">
							<b><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?action=profile;u=393" title="View the profile of Zerxes">Zerxes</a></b><br />
							<span class="smalltext">
								Splitpaw Gnoll<br />
								<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/star.gif" alt="*" border="0" /><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/star.gif" alt="*" border="0" /><br />
								Posts: 51<br />
								<br />
								<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/YaBBImages/avatars/blank.gif" alt="" border="0" /><br />
								I'm a llama!<br />
								<br />
								
								
								
								<br />
								<a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?action=profile;u=393"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/icons/profile_sm.gif" alt="View Profile" title="View Profile" border="0" /></a>
							</span>
						</td>
						<td valign="top" width="85%" height="100%">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="middle"><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1016.msg10108#msg10108"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/post/xx.gif" alt="" border="0" /></a></td>
								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1016.msg10108#msg10108">Re: Future of EQ</a></b><br />
									<span class="smalltext">&#171; <b>Reply #4 on:</b> August 16, 2004, 11:32:09 am &#187;</span></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div class="quoteheader"><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1016.msg10107#msg10107">Quote from: Delheru on August 16, 2004, 09:47:48 am</a></div><div class="quote">Of course it&#039;s quite reasonable to wonder if any game can really re-capture this original audience, and it&#039;s not so much EQ getting old, as it&#039;s the whole genre getting old.<br /></div><br />And that right there is the problem - people are desperately seeking to recapture their &quot;MMORPG virginity&quot;, and it aint going to happen.&nbsp; You lost it; quit trying to get it back.<br /><br />I&#039;ve said this before (and on this board in fact):&nbsp; When I first logged into to EQ, I was overwhelmed.&nbsp; I was in this three dimensional world with no clue as to what I was to do except a single note telling me to go see my guild master.&nbsp; Unlike single player RPGs, there was nothing guiding me and directing me along a path (usually a storyline path).<br /><br />Now, I can log into any given MMORPG and I know exactly what to do - be very observant so you don&#039;t get lost in the world, equip your sword or memorize your spells, go out and kill monsters and let the RNG take over.&nbsp; Get your levels, sell stuff to buy better spells, etc.&nbsp; There is no mystery anymore.&nbsp; In WoW, it will be the same, with the exception of go talk to those dudes with exclamation marks over their heads.&nbsp; In fact, with a beta with no NDA, it may be even worse because every spoiler site will be chuck full of all the information you ever wanted (of course, you don&#039;t have to go see those sites I suppose).<br /><br />I&#039;m not expecting Vanguard to be too revolutionary, but who knows.&nbsp; I actually view that game as a step backward.&nbsp; Back to long corpse runs.&nbsp; Back to boat rides (oh wait!&nbsp; There will be interesting encounters to make travelling fun!&nbsp; Yeah right, maybe for the first few times . . . )&nbsp; Back to guilds racing each other for static spawns in non-instanced content.&nbsp; I&#039;m not saying that any of these things are good or bad necessarily; some players will inevitably love it.<br /><br />One thing I am concerned about Vangaurd and Mr. Brad McQuaid is that they are confusing tediousness with challenge.&nbsp; Tediousness does not equal challenging content.&nbsp; Just because it will take 1-1.5 hours to regroup after a wipe does not make the encounter itself any more challenging.&nbsp; It just serves as a punishment in the form of a time-sink, and thus some would argue adds &quot;risk&quot; (which is probably true).&nbsp; But again, time-sinks as a punishment do not equal challenging content.
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" colspan="2" class="smalltext">
								</td>
							</tr><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
								</td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
									<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/ip.gif" alt="" border="0" /> Logged
								</td>
							</tr></table>
						</td>
					</tr>
				</table>
			</td></tr>
		</table>
	</td></tr>
	<tr><td style="padding: 1px 1px 0 1px;">
		<a name="msg10116"></a>
		<table cellpadding="3" cellspacing="0" border="0" width="100%">
			<tr><td class="windowbg2">
				<table width="100%" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0">
					<tr>
						<td valign="top" width="15%" rowspan="2">
							<b><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?action=profile;u=174" title="View the profile of Delheru">Delheru</a></b><br />
							<span class="smalltext">
								Hill Giant<br />
								<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/star.gif" alt="*" border="0" /><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/star.gif" alt="*" border="0" /><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/star.gif" alt="*" border="0" /><br />
								Posts: 104<br />
								<br />
								<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/YaBBImages/avatars/blank.gif" alt="" border="0" /><br />
								
								
								
								<br />
								<a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?action=profile;u=174"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/icons/profile_sm.gif" alt="View Profile" title="View Profile" border="0" /></a>
							</span>
						</td>
						<td valign="top" width="85%" height="100%">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="middle"><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1016.msg10116#msg10116"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/post/xx.gif" alt="" border="0" /></a></td>
								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1016.msg10116#msg10116">Re: Future of EQ</a></b><br />
									<span class="smalltext">&#171; <b>Reply #5 on:</b> August 16, 2004, 04:43:57 pm &#187;</span></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div class="quoteheader">Quote</div><div class="quote">I&#039;m not expecting Vanguard to be too revolutionary, but who knows.&nbsp; I actually view that game as a step backward.&nbsp; Back to long corpse runs.&nbsp; Back to boat rides (oh wait!&nbsp; There will be interesting encounters to make travelling fun!&nbsp; Yeah right, maybe for the first few times . . . )&nbsp; Back to guilds racing each other for static spawns in non-instanced content.</div><br />What I wait most from the next &quot;revolutíonary&quot; game is a truly dynamic game environment. This will require a massive AI chugging along behind the scenes of the game. Many games have tried being dynamic using only PCs and the occasional GM. It hasn&#039;t really worked, since PCs are close to chaos incarnate, and you can only afford so many GMs.<br /><br />Essentially a game where an expansion wouldn&#039;t really be needed for new content. Hell &quot;world of IRLz!&quot; would be pretty nice to do. Expansions could involve new innovations like, say, gunpowder, plate armor or whatever. This way the game world would keep fluctuating and you wouldn&#039;t have to go fight in a new dimension or the bloody moon. Not to mention it&#039;d sorta explain the mudflation and you could go an awful long way staying logical (instead of &quot;steel... mithril... uh... truesilver.. hrm... thorium.. uh uh uh... blizzardium!?&quot;). Not to mention you would have a great chance of nerfing everyone via a massive dynamic event (magic armor on everyone? Hostile force figures out a spell which sucks magic out of armor... better go get a nice yet non-magical set to fight them etc).<br /><br />Maybe a GM could have a strategy game interface to the whole thing etc. You get the idea.<br /><br /><br /><div class="quoteheader">Quote</div><div class="quote">One thing I am concerned about Vangaurd and Mr. Brad McQuaid is that they are confusing tediousness with challenge.&nbsp; Tediousness does not equal challenging content. </div><br />I think you&#039;re bit wrong there. There&#039;s no way to make very challenging content via simple maths if you throw a million players at it. They will figure it all out, and there&#039;s no way you can keep up with them. The difficulty comes from organizing the multiple people to perform the tasks. The more people are required, the more difficult it gets, except this creates the problem of zerging (and trivializing that way). However organizing 36 people with very limited tools and not very raid friendly stuff (buffing only your own group was maybe too harsh, but target group buff went too far imo) was interesting and immensily challenging.<br /><br />Some say it&#039;s &quot;tedious&quot; if they can&#039;t keep their buffs up for Nagafen. I say they fucking suck.<br /><br />The NPC encounter can NOT be all that difficult. Especially not a single NPC one. Organization and everyone knowing what to do is what&#039;s difficult.
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" colspan="2" class="smalltext">
								</td>
							</tr><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
								</td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
									<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/ip.gif" alt="" border="0" /> Logged
								</td>
							</tr></table>
						</td>
					</tr>
				</table>
			</td></tr>
		</table>
	</td></tr>
	<tr><td style="padding: 1px 1px 0 1px;">
		<a name="msg10121"></a>
		<table cellpadding="3" cellspacing="0" border="0" width="100%">
			<tr><td class="windowbg">
				<table width="100%" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0">
					<tr>
						<td valign="top" width="15%" rowspan="2">
							<b><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?action=profile;u=402" title="View the profile of Callon">Callon</a></b><br />
							<span class="smalltext">
								Drolvarg Guardian<br />
								<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/star.gif" alt="*" border="0" /><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/star.gif" alt="*" border="0" /><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/star.gif" alt="*" border="0" /><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/star.gif" alt="*" border="0" /><br />
								Posts: 320<br />
								<br />
								<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/YaBBImages/avatars/opus.gif" alt="" border="0" /><br />
								Warpig<br />
								<br />
								
								
								
								<br />
								<a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?action=profile;u=402"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/icons/profile_sm.gif" alt="View Profile" title="View Profile" border="0" /></a>
								<a href="mailto:murgen21@yahoo.com"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/email_sm.gif" alt="Email" title="Email" border="0" /></a>
							</span>
						</td>
						<td valign="top" width="85%" height="100%">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="middle"><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1016.msg10121#msg10121"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/post/xx.gif" alt="" border="0" /></a></td>
								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1016.msg10121#msg10121">Re: Future of EQ</a></b><br />
									<span class="smalltext">&#171; <b>Reply #6 on:</b> August 16, 2004, 07:58:34 pm &#187;</span></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							But solving the problem of how do i keep all the single target buffs up for long enough on a raid isn&#039;t fun for most people.  It might be fun for you, Delheru, but for most of us it&#039;s just tedious as hell, much like the long boat rides, lengthy corpse runs, etc.  If they want more people to play the game, they have to keep it challenging but also get away from the things that most players simply aren&#039;t entertained by.<br /><br /><div class="quoteheader">Quote</div><div class="quote">It just serves as a punishment in the form of a time-sink, and thus some would argue adds &quot;risk&quot; (which is probably true).  But again, time-sinks as a punishment do not equal challenging content. </div><br />Exactly.  I&#039;ve always hated the EQ concept of adding risk through timesinks that make it so you&#039;re basically not doing anything for long periods of time  (/gems!).  That just sucks.  There are other ways to add risk.  One very easy and obvious idea is to have bigger exp penalties.  People hate losing a lot of experience, so death is something they will want to avoid.  But the good thing is the penalty doesn&#039;t keep you from jumping back in and playing again without a lot of down time.  Sure you&#039;ve got experience to make up, but at least you can get back to having fun quickly.  Isn&#039;t that what the game is for?<br /><br />by the way, I&#039;ve heard rumors that EQ2 will be targeted at somewhat more casual players than EQ1.&nbsp; I don&#039;t know if there&#039;s any truth to that, but people who can&#039;t play 20 to 40 hrs a week are considered to be a huge market and they often complain, rightly or wrongly, of feeling left out in EQ1.&nbsp; Since they are also still making expansions for EQ1, I&#039;m wondering if EQ2 will be directed at more casual players and EQ1 will continue with expansions targeted more at raid guilds and long play times.
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" colspan="2" class="smalltext">
								</td>
							</tr><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
									&#171; <i>Last Edit: August 16, 2004, 08:04:50 pm by Callon</i> &#187;
								</td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
									<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/ip.gif" alt="" border="0" /> Logged
								</td>
							</tr></table>
						</td>
					</tr>
				</table>
			</td></tr>
		</table>
	</td></tr>
	<tr><td style="padding: 1px 1px 0 1px;">
		<a name="msg10123"></a>
		<table cellpadding="3" cellspacing="0" border="0" width="100%">
			<tr><td class="windowbg2">
				<table width="100%" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0">
					<tr>
						<td valign="top" width="15%" rowspan="2">
							<b><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?action=profile;u=174" title="View the profile of Delheru">Delheru</a></b><br />
							<span class="smalltext">
								Hill Giant<br />
								<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/star.gif" alt="*" border="0" /><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/star.gif" alt="*" border="0" /><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/star.gif" alt="*" border="0" /><br />
								Posts: 104<br />
								<br />
								<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/YaBBImages/avatars/blank.gif" alt="" border="0" /><br />
								
								
								
								<br />
								<a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?action=profile;u=174"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/icons/profile_sm.gif" alt="View Profile" title="View Profile" border="0" /></a>
							</span>
						</td>
						<td valign="top" width="85%" height="100%">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="middle"><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1016.msg10123#msg10123"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/post/xx.gif" alt="" border="0" /></a></td>
								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1016.msg10123#msg10123">Re: Future of EQ</a></b><br />
									<span class="smalltext">&#171; <b>Reply #7 on:</b> August 16, 2004, 08:19:14 pm &#187;</span></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							Game engines are trivial to figure out pretty fast, so it comes down to either clicking speed (reflexes, some brain) or organization.<br /><br />Originally EQ was an organizational nightmare, and something like a PoF run was challenging indeed. Every organizational problem has been &quot;fixed&quot; these days, and the difficulty certainly hasn&#039;t moved to clicking. It&#039;s becoming more and more a question of time commitment and guild HR. Even the HR is pretty damn trivial if you know how to handle it (I never had any issues), so it came down to clocking sufficient hours. That&#039;s all that EQ is atm. If you can&#039;t cut it even while playing lots, you&#039;re doing something severely wrong.
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" colspan="2" class="smalltext">
								</td>
							</tr><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
								</td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
									<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/ip.gif" alt="" border="0" /> Logged
								</td>
							</tr></table>
						</td>
					</tr>
				</table>
			</td></tr>
		</table>
	</td></tr>
	<tr><td style="padding: 1px 1px 0 1px;">
		<a name="msg10131"></a>
		<table cellpadding="3" cellspacing="0" border="0" width="100%">
			<tr><td class="windowbg">
				<table width="100%" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0">
					<tr>
						<td valign="top" width="15%" rowspan="2">
							<b><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?action=profile;u=399" title="View the profile of Donal">Donal</a></b><br />
							<span class="smalltext">
								Orc Pawn<br />
								<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/star.gif" alt="*" border="0" /><br />
								Posts: 5<br />
								<br />
								<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/YaBBImages/avatars/blank.gif" alt="" border="0" /><br />
								An Everquest Player on Rodcet Nife<br />
								<br />
								
								
								
								<br />
								<a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?action=profile;u=399"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/icons/profile_sm.gif" alt="View Profile" title="View Profile" border="0" /></a>
							</span>
						</td>
						<td valign="top" width="85%" height="100%">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="middle"><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1016.msg10131#msg10131"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/post/xx.gif" alt="" border="0" /></a></td>
								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1016.msg10131#msg10131">Re: Future of EQ</a></b><br />
									<span class="smalltext">&#171; <b>Reply #8 on:</b> August 17, 2004, 02:56:07 am &#187;</span></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div class="quoteheader"><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1016.msg10107#msg10107">Quote from: Delheru on August 16, 2004, 09:47:48 am</a></div><div class="quote">WoW doesn&#039;t even TRY to do anything revolutionary, which means it might not really hold my attention.</div><br />And this is why I stopped at level 20. <br /><br />I&#039;ve played a few games since I started with EQ.<br />The first to hold my attention for any length of time (about a month and a little change), oddly enough, was Earth and Beyond. But, they, like so many others, had a nicely fleshed out low level game, and when you got to high levels it was glaringly obvious they put no effort into designing for that. Also, like so many others, the devs didn&#039;t spent a lot of time attempting to fix fairly significant issues. Welcome to bankruptcy. <br /><br />The second was DAoC. I had tried it when it came out but wasn&#039;t entirely enthralled. Tried it again a while later and had a blast with it. DAoC&#039;s real trick was giving the players other players to fight against. PVP games, in my mind, have the greatest potential for long term entertainment provided they&#039;re done correctly. So very few have actually come up with a good pvp system though. I liked DAoC&#039;s system for the fact that it was on your terms if you felt like fighting with others. I just didn&#039;t like the fact (and this falls back into the design section) that a whole lot of it came down to min/maxing. However, the fact that you&#039;re playing against other people makes up for a great deal as many of them aren&#039;t simple AIs who are easy to predict. Yeah, there are a significant number who aren&#039;t very challenging or creative, but there are enough good people out there to give you at least some good fights.<br /><br />The third was UT2004. Slightly more in the realm of pvp, heh. Played with a clan for a while, but started getting burnt out from some of our better players not being around enough. The skill level thing is so drastic in that game compared to an rpg. I don&#039;t wish to fuel any kind of fps vs rpg thing as they&#039;re very different animals and require different kinds of play, but missing a top player there was just a lot more painful. <br />Still though, the team games there require much tighter and faster teamwork than something like EQ. You are far more reliant on teammates who can adapt to situations. EQ, really, you only need a few out of the raid force who need to adapt, and the rest need to be able to follow orders quickly. I also appreciated the fact that I could log on or off at just about any time, and the most time any ladder match ever took was about 90 minutes for everyone to log in, warm up, get onto whichever server for the match, and play. <br /><br />WoW, unfortunately, just never really struck my fancy. There&#039;s a lot of amazingly cool stuff in it, but as Del stated, it&#039;s nothing truly revolutionary. After hitting 20, I just was generally annoyed with playing it. I figure I&#039;ve leveled up enough characters in enough different games at this point, and as stated above as well, the magic just isn&#039;t there. I know what to expect and I know how to generally handle myself in any game I step into. The only real learning is the basic understanding of how this different engine works, and information about the world itself. <br /><br />EDIT: As for what all this means for the future of EQ. Eh, it means a bunch of people will take off, and a bunch will stay. For many folks, playing with the usual gang is the fun part. It&#039;s part of the reason I ended up leaving DAoC. Had my normal crew stayed, I&#039;d likely still be there. For the individuals out there, it just varies on the person. I suspect quite a few will head off to another game since they don&#039;t really have anything holding them in EQ.
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" colspan="2" class="smalltext">
								</td>
							</tr><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
									&#171; <i>Last Edit: August 17, 2004, 03:42:20 am by Donal</i> &#187;
								</td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" class="smalltext">
									<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/ip.gif" alt="" border="0" /> Logged
								</td>
							</tr></table>
						</td>
					</tr>
				</table>
			</td></tr>
		</table>
	</td></tr>
	<tr><td style="padding: 1px 1px 0 1px;">
		<a name="msg10132"></a>
		<table cellpadding="3" cellspacing="0" border="0" width="100%">
			<tr><td class="windowbg2">
				<table width="100%" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0">
					<tr>
						<td valign="top" width="15%" rowspan="2">
							<b><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?action=profile;u=174" title="View the profile of Delheru">Delheru</a></b><br />
							<span class="smalltext">
								Hill Giant<br />
								<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/star.gif" alt="*" border="0" /><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/star.gif" alt="*" border="0" /><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/star.gif" alt="*" border="0" /><br />
								Posts: 104<br />
								<br />
								<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/YaBBImages/avatars/blank.gif" alt="" border="0" /><br />
								
								
								
								<br />
								<a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?action=profile;u=174"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/icons/profile_sm.gif" alt="View Profile" title="View Profile" border="0" /></a>
							</span>
						</td>
						<td valign="top" width="85%" height="100%">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="middle"><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1016.msg10132#msg10132"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/post/xx.gif" alt="" border="0" /></a></td>
								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1016.msg10132#msg10132">Re: Future of EQ</a></b><br />
									<span class="smalltext">&#171; <b>Reply #9 on:</b> August 17, 2004, 05:42:13 am &#187;</span></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div class="quoteheader">Quote</div><div class="quote"> For many folks, playing with the usual gang is the fun part.</div><br />Yeah until something truly revolutionary happens, this is the main thing. Only one rather out of the question is EQ, since it takes too much time to play, and that time needs to be committed at exactly the wrong time for me.<br /><br />As it is, majority of my close friends are in WoW at the moment, and as such, WoW is quite preferably. The world is fascinating and some of the quests have had serious effort put in to them. I also knew Furor, and he&#039;s been doing the high end quests... I&#039;m rather inclined to give him a shot at walking the walk. We&#039;ll see in two pushes I guess.
						</td>
					</tr>
