							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="middle"><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1016.msg10153#msg10153"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/post/xx.gif" alt="" border="0" /></a></td>
								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1016.msg10153#msg10153">Re: Future of EQ</a></b><br />
									<span class="smalltext">&#171; <b>Reply #11 on:</b> August 17, 2004, 02:14:11 pm &#187;</span></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							One thing I think Sony does well is adapt.&nbsp; If elementals get opened up by level requirements and the upper level llimit is raised to 70,&nbsp; I think sony has basically catered to hard core guilds and casual gamers alike.&nbsp; &nbsp;Add in solo ldons and eq remains versitle.&nbsp; 
